DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     Named extended attributes are meta-data associated with vnodes represent‐
     ing files and directories.  They exist as "name=value" pairs within a set
     of namespaces.

     The eexxttaattttrr__ggeett__ffiillee() system call retrieves the value of the specified
     extended attribute into a buffer pointed to by _d_a_t_a of size _n_b_y_t_e_s.  The
     eexxttaattttrr__sseett__ffiillee() system call sets the value of the specified extended
     attribute to the data described by _d_a_t_a.  The eexxttaattttrr__ddeelleettee__ffiillee() sys‐
     tem call deletes the extended attribute specified.  The
     eexxttaattttrr__lliisstt__ffiillee() returns a list of attributes present in the requested
     namespace.  Each list entry consists of a single byte containing the
     length of the attribute name, followed by the attribute name.  The attri‐
     bute name is not terminated by ASCII 0 (nul).  The eexxttaattttrr__ggeett__ffiillee() and
     eexxttaattttrr__lliisstt__ffiillee() calls consume the _d_a_t_a and _n_b_y_t_e_s arguments in the
     style of read(2); eexxttaattttrr__sseett__ffiillee() consumes these arguments in the
     style of write(2).

     If _d_a_t_a is NULL in a call to eexxttaattttrr__ggeett__ffiillee() and eexxttaattttrr__lliisstt__ffiillee()
     then the size of defined extended attribute data will be returned, rather
     than the quantity read, permitting applications to test the size of the
     data without performing a read.  The eexxttaattttrr__ddeelleettee__lliinnkk(),
     eexxttaattttrr__ggeett__lliinnkk(), and eexxttaattttrr__sseett__lliinnkk() system calls behave in the
     same way as their _file counterparts, except that they do not follow sym‐
     links.

     The eexxttaattttrr__ggeett__ffdd(), eexxttaattttrr__ddeelleettee__ffdd(), eexxttaattttrr__lliisstt__ffdd(), and
     eexxttaattttrr__sseett__ffdd() calls are identical to their "_file" counterparts except
     for the first argument.  The "_fd" functions take a file descriptor,
     while the "_file" functions take a path.  Both arguments describe a file
     associated with the extended attribute that should be manipulated.  The
     "_fd" functions can be used with file descriptors opened with the O_PATH
     flag.

     The following arguments are common to all the system calls described
     here:

     _a_t_t_r_n_a_m_e_s_p_a_c_e  the namespace in which the extended attribute resides; see
                    extattr(9)

     _a_t_t_r_n_a_m_e       the name of the extended attribute

     Named extended attribute semantics vary by file system implementing the
     call.  Not all operations may be supported for a particular attribute.
     Additionally, the format of the data in _d_a_t_a is attribute-specific.

     For more information on named extended attributes, please see extattr(9).

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     If successful, the eexxttaattttrr__ggeett__ffdd(), eexxttaattttrr__ggeett__ffiillee(),
     eexxttaattttrr__ggeett__lliinnkk(), eexxttaattttrr__lliisstt__ffdd(), eexxttaattttrr__lliisstt__ffiillee(),
     eexxttaattttrr__lliisstt__lliinnkk(), eexxttaattttrr__sseett__ffdd(), eexxttaattttrr__sseett__ffiillee(), and
     eexxttaattttrr__sseett__lliinnkk() calls return the number of bytes that were read or
     written from the _d_a_t_a, respectively.  If _d_a_t_a was NULL, then
     eexxttaattttrr__ggeett__ffdd(), eexxttaattttrr__ggeett__ffiillee(), eexxttaattttrr__ggeett__lliinnkk(),
     eexxttaattttrr__lliisstt__ffdd(), eexxttaattttrr__lliisstt__ffiillee(), and eexxttaattttrr__lliisstt__lliinnkk() return
     the number of bytes available to read.  If any of the calls are unsuc‐
     cessful, the value -1 is returned and the global variable _e_r_r_n_o is set to
     indicate the error.

     The eexxttaattttrr__ddeelleettee__ffiillee() function returns the value 0 if successful;
     otherwise the value -1 is returned and the global variable _e_r_r_n_o is set
     to indicate the error.

EERRRROORRSS
     The following errors may be returned by the system calls themselves.  Ad‐
     ditionally, the file system implementing the call may return any other
     errors it desires.

     [EFAULT]           The _a_t_t_r_n_a_m_e_s_p_a_c_e and _a_t_t_r_n_a_m_e arguments, or the mem‐
                        ory range defined by _d_a_t_a and _n_b_y_t_e_s point outside the
                        process's allocated address space.

     [ENAMETOOLONG]     The attribute name was longer than EXTATTR_MAXNAMELEN.

     The eexxttaattttrr__ggeett__ffdd(), eexxttaattttrr__sseett__ffdd(), eexxttaattttrr__ddeelleettee__ffdd(), and
     eexxttaattttrr__lliisstt__ffdd() system calls may also fail if:

     [EBADF]            The file descriptor referenced by _f_d was invalid.

     Additionally, the eexxttaattttrr__ggeett__ffiillee(), eexxttaattttrr__sseett__ffiillee(), and
     eexxttaattttrr__ddeelleettee__ffiillee() calls may also fail due to the following errors:

     [ENOATTR]          The requested attribute was not defined for this file.

     [ENOTDIR]          A component of the path prefix is not a directory.

     [ENAMETOOLONG]     A component of a pathname exceeded 255 characters, or
                        an entire path name exceeded 1023 characters.

     [ENOENT]           A component of the path name that must exist does not
                        exist.

     [EACCES]           Search permission is denied for a component of the
                        path prefix.

